2026-11-03 17:27 Decisions (mine, verksted-worker), verksted/paths.py:
- Every check runs through an injectable Backend (path flavour + fs calls of the process that executes the tool); tests inject ntpath + fakefs.WinFS (simulated with ntpath — not exercised on Windows).
- Canonical form: realpath strict (symlinks, junctions, 8.3 names as the OS expands them), then isdir. No Unicode normalisation either way (NFC/NFD stay different names); a name that is not valid Unicode is refused `bad_folder` (cannot be stored or sent).
- Windows: drive paths only. \\?\X:\ accepted and stored WITHOUT the prefix (Hermes' tools need the plain form); a folder reachable only with the prefix → `path_too_long`. UNC (\\server\share, //server, \\?\UNC\, \\wsl.localhost\, \\localhost\C$) → `unc_path`; \\.\ and \\?\Volume{…} → `device_path`; a mapped network drive whose realpath is UNC → `unc_path` (v1 = lokale Arbeitsordner, §7). Drive-relative (C:x), root-relative (\x), relative → `bad_folder`.
- Lock key (one active run per folder): normcase of the canonical path (case-folded on Windows); on a WSL backend /mnt/<drive>/ paths are lower-cased too (drvfs is case-insensitive by default), Linux paths stay case-sensitive.
- WSL: only when hermes_constants.is_wsl() — a Windows path is mapped with Hermes' translate_cwd_for_wsl_backend and the mapped path is then checked like any other; \\wsl.localhost|wsl$\<distro> must name WSL_DISTRO_NAME (Hermes' wsl_unc_path_to_posix ignores the distro); anything unmappable → `windows_path`. Off WSL a Windows path on a Linux backend → `windows_path`, never passed on.
- files: ≤50 relative paths; absolute, drive or rooted entries refused; both the lexical normpath and the link-resolved path must stay inside the canonical folder → `bad_files`.
- docs/windows-acceptance.md: new section G–K (drive-letter case, space+umlaut+files, junction into/out of the workspace incl. the registration refusal, \\?\ and long paths, WSL backend given C:\), each with steps, expected observation and the §7 row; A–F untouched; 'WSL path spaces (k6)' dropped from 'Not covered here'.
